CONTENT = {
"password-strength-checker": {
"intro": [
"Most people judge a password by how it feels, and feelings are a terrible security instrument. A password that looks long and complicated can still fall in minutes if it is built from predictable patterns, while a shorter-looking string of truly random characters can take centuries to exhaust. This checker replaces guesswork with arithmetic: it measures the length of your password, the size of the character pool it draws from, and computes the entropy that an attacker would actually have to defeat. Nothing you type is transmitted or stored; the analysis runs entirely inside your browser tab.",
"The number that matters is entropy, measured in bits. Each bit doubles the number of guesses an attacker must make on average, so the scale is exponential rather than linear. A password with 28 bits of entropy is a joke; 60 bits is respectable for most accounts; 80 bits and above is genuinely hard to attack even with serious hardware. The checker also converts that entropy into an estimated offline crack time, which turns abstract bits into a sentence you can act on.",
"Strength is not only about randomness. Reused passwords, dictionary words, keyboard walks and personal details all undermine an otherwise long password, because attackers try those patterns first and in enormous volumes. The tool runs structural checks, flags the weaknesses it finds, and explains which change would raise your strength the most, so you leave with a fix and not just a score.",
],
"how": [
"Type or paste any password into the analysis box and the meter, entropy figure, crack-time estimate and checklist update as you type. Use a test password of the same shape and habits as your real one rather than the real thing itself; the arithmetic that governs a pattern is identical whether or not the exact characters are the ones you actually use. Nothing leaves the page, so even pasting a real password is technically private, but testing patterns is the smarter habit.",
],
"steps": [
"Open the Password Strength Checker page.",
"Click into the analysis box and type a candidate password.",
"Watch the coloured meter move as length and character variety change.",
"Read the entropy figure, shown in bits, above the checklist.",
"Check the estimated offline crack time for a serious attacker.",
"Review the checklist items and fix the first unchecked weakness.",
"Adjust the password until the meter reaches the strong band.",
"Repeat with your other important passwords as patterns, not verbatim.",
],
"works": [
"The entropy calculation starts by identifying which character classes your password uses. Lowercase letters contribute 26 possible symbols, uppercase another 26, digits 10, and common keyboard symbols roughly 33. The size of the pool is raised to the power of the password length to get the total search space, and the base-two logarithm of that number is the entropy in bits. Length multiplies, variety raises the base, which is why a twenty-character lowercase passphrase can outrank an eight-character soup of symbols.",
"The crack-time estimate converts entropy into hours, days or centuries using an assumed attack rate of ten billion guesses per second, a realistic figure for an attacker with a rack of modern graphics cards against a stolen database. Real outcomes vary with the site's hashing scheme, but the relative comparison between passwords is honest: doubling the entropy multiplies the attack time exponentially.",
"Structural checks catch what raw entropy misses. The tool tests for length below twelve characters, the absence of any digit or symbol, sequences like abc or 123, repeated characters, and a small internal list of the most commonly leaked passwords and their close variants. Each failed check appears with a plain-language explanation, and the meter refuses to show the strong band while a critical weakness remains, because a password that appears on every breach list is not strong at all.",
],
"examples": [
("Testing a typical weak password", "A password like Summer2024! scores lower than most people expect. It has upper case, lower case, digits and a symbol, so the pool looks healthy, but the structure is a capitalised dictionary word plus a year plus an exclamation mark, one of the most common shapes in every breach analysis ever published. The checker flags the pattern, and the crack time lands in hours rather than millennia."),
("Comparing a passphrase", "A four-word random passphrase such as lantern-copper-mirth-quilt reaches a similar or better entropy figure with far less mental overhead. Watching the meter respond as you add each word makes the mathematics visible: every random word added at that length contributes more bits than another symbol ever could."),
("Tuning an existing password", "If your favourite password scores medium, add four truly random characters rather than replacing letters with lookalike symbols. The entropy readout rises immediately and honestly, while tricks like swapping a for @ add almost nothing because attackers try those substitutions first."),
],
"mistakes": [
"Trusting complexity rules over length. A short password forced through symbol requirements is still exhaustible; a long random one is not. Length is the cheapest entropy you can buy.",
"Substituting lookalike characters and assuming safety. Attackers know every standard substitution and test them automatically, so a for @ and s for $ add almost no real strength.",
"Reusing a strong password everywhere. Strength does not survive reuse; one breached site hands attackers your credential for every other site where it was reused.",
"Ignoring the checklist because the meter looks green. The meter is conservative on purpose; a green meter with an unchecked warning still describes a password worth improving.",
],
"tips": [
"Aim for at least fourteen characters with all four character classes represented.",
"Prefer random passphrases of four or more unrelated words; they are easier to remember and harder to attack.",
"Use a password manager and let it generate passwords you never need to memorise.",
"Never base a password on your name, birth year, team, or any string found on your social profiles.",
"Change passwords that have appeared in a breach, not on a schedule; rotation without cause encourages weaker choices.",
"Enable two-factor authentication everywhere it exists; it rescues even a mediocre password from most attacks.",
],
"faqs": [
("Does this tool send my password anywhere?", "No. The entire analysis, including the entropy math, crack-time estimate and checklist, runs in your browser with JavaScript. No network request is made with what you type."),
("What is a good entropy score?", "Sixty bits or more is a reasonable target for important accounts, and eighty bits or more is excellent. Below about forty bits, an offline attacker with commodity hardware is a genuine threat."),
("Why does my long password still score medium?", "Length alone is not enough if the content is predictable. Repeated characters, sequences, keyboard walks and common words all reduce effective entropy, and the checklist explains exactly which pattern is costing you bits."),
("How accurate is the crack-time estimate?", "It assumes a fast offline attack at ten billion guesses per second against a stolen hash database. Real outcomes depend on the site's hashing, but the relative comparison between passwords is reliable."),
("Is a passphrase really better than a random password?", "A random passphrase of four or more words delivers comparable entropy with far better memorability, so you are more likely to keep it unique. That practical edge usually makes it the better choice."),
],
},
"cron-expression-explainer": {
"intro": [
"A cron expression is five small fields that together describe a schedule, and it is one of the most compact languages in computing. It is also one of the easiest to get wrong, because a single misplaced character silently changes a job from every hour to once a year. This explainer takes any standard five-field expression and translates it into plain English, field by field, so you can see exactly when your job will run before it surprises you at three in the morning.",
"Beyond translation, the tool computes the next scheduled run times from the current clock. Reading a description tells you what an expression means in principle; seeing the next five concrete timestamps tells you what it means in practice, including the subtle interactions between the day-of-month and day-of-week fields that trip up even experienced administrators. If the next runs are not what you expected, the expression is wrong, and now you know before production does.",
"The explainer runs entirely in your browser and follows the classic cron conventions used by Linux crontab and most job schedulers: five fields for minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week, with asterisks, ranges, steps, lists and the day-of-week and month names accepted. Nothing is uploaded, so it is safe to paste expressions from private infrastructure.",
],
"how": [
"Paste or type a five-field cron expression into the input and the explanation appears immediately, with each field broken out in order and the next scheduled executions listed below. Adjust the expression and the reading updates live, which makes it a fast way to experiment: change a step from every fifteen minutes to every five and watch the next-run list densify. If a field is invalid, the tool says which field and why rather than failing silently.",
],
"steps": [
"Open the Cron Expression Explainer page.",
"Type a five-field expression such as every fifteen minutes of the working weekday.",
"Read the plain-English breakdown of each field in order.",
"Check the list of next run times computed from the current time.",
"Confirm the runs match your intention, especially the day fields.",
"Adjust ranges or steps until the next-run list is exactly right.",
"Copy the validated expression back into your crontab or scheduler.",
],
"works": [
"Each field is parsed independently against its own grammar. A field can be an asterisk meaning every unit, a single number, a range like nine to seventeen, a step meaning every fifteenth unit, a list such as zero, fifteen, thirty and forty-five, or named values like MON for weekdays. The explainer resolves each component and renders it as a sentence, so five asterisks read as every minute of every day.",
"The next-run calculation walks forward from the current minute, minute by minute, testing each candidate timestamp against all five fields until it accumulates five matches. That brute-force approach is simple and faithful to how cron itself behaves, including the classic rule that when both day-of-month and day-of-week are restricted, a run happens when either one matches. The walk is capped so impossible expressions, like February thirtieth, report no future runs instead of hanging.",
"Field interaction is where the explainer earns its keep. The pair of a star day-of-month with weekdays in day-of-week means weekdays, but first and fifteenth in day-of-month with Monday in day-of-week means both the first and the fifteenth and every Monday. Descriptions plus concrete next-run timestamps make those interactions visible, which is precisely the class of bug that otherwise surfaces as a mysterious double run or a silent skip.",
],
"examples": [
("The weekday working-hours classic", "An expression running every fifteen minutes between nine and five, Monday to Friday, is the most common production schedule in existence. The explainer shows the next five such minutes, confirming the schedule before you paste it into a live crontab."),
("Monthly report job", "A schedule of zero minutes past six in the morning on the first of every month is written with zero in the minute field and six in the hour field. Seeing the next run listed as the first of next month at six catches the common mistake of writing the hour value in the minute position."),
("Diagnosing a surprising schedule", "An expression with both day-of-month and day-of-week restricted often runs more often than intended. The next-run list exposes the either-or behaviour immediately, turning a confusing cron rule into an obvious fact."),
],
"mistakes": [
"Swapping the minute and hour fields. The first field is minutes, the second is hours; zero then nine means nine in the morning, while nine then zero means nine minutes past every hour.",
"Forgetting that day-of-month and day-of-week combine with OR when both are restricted. Restrict both only when you truly want either condition to trigger a run.",
"Using a zero step or a reversed range. Steps must be positive and ranges must ascend, or the field is invalid.",
"Assuming the scheduler's timezone matches yours. Cron runs in the machine's local time; the next-run list here uses your browser clock, so confirm server timezones separately.",
"Nesting named and numeric values inconsistently. Mixing MON with six in one field is valid but confusing; pick one style per expression.",
],
"tips": [
"Validate every expression here before adding it to a live crontab.",
"Prefer explicit ranges over bare asterisks in the hour field to keep jobs inside working hours.",
"Use step values for high-frequency jobs instead of long comma lists; they read faster and edit safer.",
"Remember field order with the phrase minutes, hours, day, month, weekday.",
"When a job must run on a specific date regardless of weekday, restrict day-of-month and leave day-of-week as star.",
],
"faqs": [
("Which cron dialect does the tool support?", "The standard five-field POSIX-style syntax used by Linux crontab, including ranges, steps, lists and month or weekday names. Six-field variants with seconds are not part of classic cron and are not supported."),
("Why do the next runs use my local time?", "The tool computes from your browser clock so the timestamps match what you see on your watch. Your server may run in a different timezone; check it before scheduling anything timezone-sensitive."),
("Can I use it for expressions from Kubernetes CronJobs?", "Yes. Kubernetes CronJobs use the same five-field syntax, so the explanation and next-run list apply directly. Systemd timers use a different OnCalendar syntax, which this tool does not parse."),
("What does a step of five in the minute field mean?", "It means every fifth minute: 00, 05, 10 and so on through 55, every hour that the remaining fields allow."),
("Why does my expression show no next runs?", "The fields describe a moment that never occurs, such as February thirtieth, or a combination the OR rule never satisfies. Re-read the field breakdown to find the impossible constraint."),
("Does the tool store or send my expression?", "No. Parsing and the next-run walk happen entirely in your browser, and nothing is transmitted or logged."),
],
},
"css-gradient-generator": {
"intro": [
"Gradients are the fastest way to add depth and polish to a web page, and CSS makes them a one-line affair once you can speak their syntax. The trouble is that nobody speaks it fluently: angles, colour stops, hard stops and multiple layers are easy to miswrite and tedious to tune by hand. This generator gives you two colour pickers, an angle dial and a live preview that updates as you drag, then hands you the exact CSS declaration to paste into your stylesheet.",
"The preview is the real interface. Instead of imagining what a diagonal fade from orange to violet looks like, you see it at full width instantly, and every adjustment of the angle slider or either colour repaints the canvas in real time. Below the preview, the generated gradient declaration sits in a copy-ready block, formatted exactly as modern CSS expects it, including the degree value and hex colours.",
"Because everything is computed in the browser, the tool is instant and private, and the output it produces is framework-agnostic: the same declaration works in a plain stylesheet, a utility-class arbitrary value, a CSS-in-JS object or a design-token file. Whether you are building a hero banner, a button hover state or a subtle card background, the workflow is the same: pick, drag, copy.",
],
"how": [
"Choose your starting and ending colours with the two colour pickers, drag the angle slider until the preview looks right, and copy the generated CSS. The angle is measured in the standard CSS convention where zero degrees points up and ninety degrees points right, so the slider maps one-to-one with what browsers render. For a two-colour fade you need nothing else; the tool keeps the output minimal and valid.",
],
"steps": [
"Open the CSS Gradient Generator page.",
"Pick the first colour with the left colour picker.",
"Pick the second colour with the right colour picker.",
"Drag the angle slider and watch the preview rotate live.",
"Fine-tune until the transition sits where you want it.",
"Copy the generated CSS from the output block.",
"Paste the declaration into your stylesheet as a background or background-image value.",
],
"works": [
"A linear gradient is defined by a direction and a list of colour stops. The generator writes the direction as an angle in degrees, which is more predictable than keyword directions like toward the bottom right because the angle is continuous and identical across browsers. The two colours become stops at the default positions, zero percent and one hundred percent, which lets the browser distribute the blend evenly across the element.",
"Hex colours are emitted in the compact modern form the pickers provide, and they are interchangeable with rgb, hsl or any other CSS colour format if you prefer to edit them by hand. The generated declaration is a plain background-image value, so it layers correctly with background colours, images and blend modes without any special handling.",
"The angle convention has one famous wrinkle that the preview eliminates: in CSS, zero degrees points upward and angles increase clockwise, which is the opposite of the mathematical convention most people learned. Dragging the slider while watching the preview teaches the convention in seconds, and the copy button guarantees the number you settled on is the number that ships.",
],
"examples": [
("A hero banner background", "A deep navy to warm orange gradient at around 160 degrees gives a landing-page hero a sense of light and atmosphere with a single declaration. Copy the output, paste it as the hero's background, and layer white text over it."),
("A subtle card wash", "Two nearly identical colours a few percent apart in lightness produce a barely-there gradient that lifts cards off flat backgrounds without shouting. Set both pickers to neighbouring shades and keep the angle at 145 degrees."),
("A button hover state", "Generate a brighter variant of your button gradient, then apply the first gradient normally and the second on hover with a short transition. The two copied declarations become an instant polished interaction."),
],
"mistakes": [
"Forgetting that zero degrees points up, not right. If your gradient runs the wrong way, the angle convention is almost always the reason.",
"Choosing two colours with identical lightness. Gradients between colours of very different lightness read cleanly; equal-lightness pairs can look muddy or vibrate.",
"Pasting the gradient into a color property instead of background or background-image. Gradients are images in CSS, not colours.",
"Stacking a gradient over an image without transparency. The gradient will fully cover the image unless at least one stop uses rgba or a transparency keyword.",
"Hard-coding the gradient in a dozen places. Put the declaration in a custom property or design token and reference it once.",
],
"tips": [
"Keep hero gradients between about 120 and 200 degrees for a natural top-lit feel.",
"Use at most two or three stops; more stops usually read as noise rather than design.",
"Test the gradient behind real text to confirm contrast survives at both ends.",
"Save your favourite gradient declarations in a snippet file for reuse across projects.",
"Pair a gradient background with a subtle inner border for a premium, layered look.",
],
"faqs": [
("Can I use more than two colours?", "The generator outputs the classic two-stop fade, but the copied declaration is ordinary CSS, so you can add more stops by hand, with a middle colour pinned at the fifty percent mark."),
("Do gradients work in every browser?", "Linear gradients with the angle syntax are supported by every browser in current use, including mobile. No prefixes are needed for modern targets."),
("Why does my gradient look different on another screen?", "Wide-gamut displays render saturated colours more vividly. Keep important text contrast high enough that the design survives the difference."),
("Can I make a radial gradient instead?", "This tool focuses on linear gradients. The same colour logic applies to radial gradients, which you can write by swapping the function name and direction for a shape and position."),
("Is the generated CSS minified or pretty?", "It is emitted as a single clean declaration, ready to paste into any stylesheet or inline style attribute without reformatting."),
("Does the tool store my colours?", "No. Everything is computed locally in your browser and nothing is uploaded or saved."),
],
},
"box-shadow-generator": {
"intro": [
"Shadows are what separate flat, lifeless interfaces from ones that feel physical, and CSS box-shadow is the property that draws them. The syntax is short but fiddly: horizontal offset, vertical offset, blur, spread and colour all interact, and tuning them by editing numbers and refreshing is a slow way to design. This generator replaces the edit-refresh loop with sliders and a live preview, then writes the exact declaration for you to copy.",
"Every slider maps directly to one component of the shadow, so the preview teaches you the property's anatomy while you use it. Horizontal offset slides the shadow left or right, vertical offset moves it up or down, blur softens the edge, spread grows or shrinks it, and the colour picker with an opacity control sets the tone. A single checkbox flips the shadow to inset, drawing it inside the element for pressed or well effects.",
"The output is a plain box-shadow declaration using modern rgba colour syntax, valid in every browser and every framework. Because the preview element is a plain rounded card on the page's own background, what you see is what your components will look like in context, which makes it easy to match shadows to the rest of your design system.",
],
"how": [
"Adjust the sliders until the preview card sits the way you want, tick inset only if you want an internal shadow, then copy the generated CSS. Small offsets with generous blur produce the soft, modern look; zero offsets with small blur create crisp definition; large negative spreads pull a shadow tight against the element. The declaration updates character for character as you drag, so the copy button always reflects the current preview.",
],
"steps": [
"Open the Box Shadow Generator page.",
"Set the horizontal offset with the first slider.",
"Set the vertical offset with the second slider.",
"Adjust blur to control how soft the shadow's edge is.",
"Adjust spread to grow or shrink the shadow before blurring.",
"Pick the shadow colour and set its opacity.",
"Tick the inset box for an internal shadow, if needed.",
"Copy the generated declaration into your stylesheet.",
],
"works": [
"The box-shadow value is a tuple: horizontal offset, vertical offset, blur radius, spread radius and colour, optionally preceded by the inset keyword. Positive horizontal offset pushes the shadow right and positive vertical offset pushes it down, matching the direction of a light source above and to the left, which is the default mental model in most UI design. Blur defines how many pixels beyond the shadow's edge the fade extends, and spread expands or contracts the shadow silhouette before that fade is applied.",
"Colour is emitted as rgba so the opacity slider has a numeric home in the declaration. Semi-transparent blacks, around ten to twenty percent opacity, are the workhorse for neutral elevation; tinted shadows that borrow the page's accent hue read as more designed. Very low opacities with large blurs create ambient depth, while higher opacities with tight blurs read as hard drop shadows from a close light.",
"Inset shadows invert the geometry, drawing the shadow within the element's border box. They are the standard way to suggest pressed states, sunken input wells and track grooves, and they stack cleanly with an outer shadow on the same element, which is how realistic buttons combine a raised rest state with a pressed state.",
],
"examples": [
("A modern elevated card", "Vertical offset around ten pixels, blur thirty, spread negative eight, black at eighteen percent. That single declaration is the soft floating look used by nearly every contemporary dashboard card."),
("A pressed button state", "Switch to inset with a small downward offset and low blur to make a button look physically pushed in. Pair it with the raised outer shadow as the default state for a satisfying toggle."),
("A coloured glow", "Set both offsets to zero, spread to a few pixels, and choose your brand colour at forty percent opacity. The result is a halo that works beautifully for focused inputs and active toggles."),
],
"mistakes": [
"Using full-opacity black shadows. Real shadows are translucent; solid black reads as a sticker outline rather than depth.",
"Applying one giant shadow to everything. Depth is hierarchical; larger shadows should mean higher elevation, used sparingly.",
"Forgetting that spread expands before blur. A large spread with large blur can bleed shadows past container edges and create clipping surprises.",
"Shadowing elements with transparent backgrounds. The shadow shows through the element itself; give the element a solid or semi-opaque background first.",
"Ignoring performance with dozens of blurred shadows on animated elements. Animate transform and opacity instead of re-rendering heavy shadows each frame.",
],
"tips": [
"Start from a neutral recipe: zero horizontal, small vertical, generous blur, slightly negative spread, black at low opacity.",
"Match shadow direction across the whole interface; mixed light directions look broken.",
"Use tinted shadows drawn from your brand hue for a subtle signature look.",
"Combine a tight shadow with a wide ambient shadow on the same element for realistic elevation.",
"Test shadows in both light and dark themes; dark themes usually need stronger, more coloured shadows to stay visible.",
],
"faqs": [
("What order do the box-shadow values follow?", "Horizontal offset first, then vertical, then blur, then spread, then colour, with the optional inset keyword in front. The generated declaration always follows that order."),
("Can I have multiple shadows on one element?", "Yes, comma-separate them, and they paint first to last. The generator outputs one shadow, but the copied value is ordinary CSS you can stack."),
("Why is my shadow not visible?", "The most common causes are a shadow colour at zero opacity, an element with a transparent background over a same-coloured page, or offsets and blur all at zero."),
("Does inset work with border-radius?", "Yes. Inset shadows follow the element's rounded corners, which is exactly why they are the standard pressed-state treatment for rounded buttons."),
("Are box shadows expensive to render?", "Large blurred shadows are one of the heavier paint operations. For animated elements, prefer transforming a pre-rendered shadow layer or animating opacity rather than the shadow itself."),
("Does the generator store my settings?", "No. All computation and preview rendering happen locally in your browser, and nothing is uploaded."),
],
},
}
